## Web Source Reading (this repo)

When reading any web page (official docs, release notes, READMEs, issues,
blog posts, tutorials, Stack Overflow, anything), use the Chrome DevTools
MCP skill (`chrome-devtools`) instead of WebFetch. There is no "low-stakes"
exemption (user rule `web-sources.md`, source aitools
`shared/rules/web-sources.md`).

### Why

WebFetch converts HTML to markdown via a smaller model -- two layers of
information loss (rendering + summarization). Official docs sites are
often JS-rendered SPAs with tabbed interfaces and platform toggles that
WebFetch misses. When accuracy matters, use the real browser.

### When to use Chrome DevTools MCP skill

- Reading official tool docs to extract install commands for the `/tool-registry` skill
- Verifying config requirements, post-install steps, or dependencies
- Checking release notes or changelogs for lifecycle field updates
- Any page whose content will be recorded verbatim in a source-of-truth file

Firecrawl is deprecated: do not use its MCP tools or skills for any web
reading, even when they appear in the tool list (commander decision;
`/delegate` skill standing orders).

### Fallback (only when the browser cannot be used)

Finding pages is web search (WebSearch), not page reading. Use WebFetch
only when the Chrome DevTools MCP is not available in the session, or
fails on the page after a retry. Then say so (which URL, why), treat the
result as a lead, not a fact, and re-read it in the browser before
recording it in a source-of-truth file.

### Cross-reference

Before reading a tool's docs, check via the `/tool-registry` skill first --
it may already have verified information. If proposing changes to an existing
entry, re-verify using Chrome DevTools MCP skill before editing.
